{"title":"Ishika's Dancing Compass at the Market","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Magnets and their poles","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was at the weekly market with her grandmother, helping to arrange iron filings in neat piles on the spice vendor's table. The vendor had a special trick: he used a magnet to separate iron dust from his masala blend."},{"label":"problem","text":"'Grandmother, why does one end of the magnet pull the iron so strongly, but when I flip it around, it seems to push the iron away?' Ishika asked, confused. The vendor laughed. 'Ah, you've found the secret of the poles!'"},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"The vendor showed Ishika that every magnet has two ends, called the poles—the North pole and the South pole. 'When you bring the North pole close to iron, it pulls with all its might. When you flip to the South pole, it still pulls the iron, but each pole pulls differently. The iron filings bunch up differently at each end because each pole has its own strength,' he explained, showing how the iron clustered thickly near one end and thinned out near the other."},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Ishika tried it herself: she held the magnet's North pole over a pile of iron filings and watched them leap up like tiny dancers. Then she flipped it, and the South pole attracted them just as eagerly, but the pattern changed. 'The poles are like two different hands on the magnet—both can hold things, but each one has its own special way!' she said happily, now understanding why her grandmother's cooking magnet worked the same way every time in the kitchen."}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"You find a bar magnet. One end is the North pole, the other is the South pole. If you hold the North pole near a paperclip, it sticks tight. What do you think will happen if you flip the magnet and hold the South pole near the same paperclip?","answer":"The South pole will also attract the paperclip, because both poles of a magnet can pull magnetic things like iron and steel."},"parentPrompt":"Find a fridge magnet at home and let Ishika turn it around to feel how both ends attract metal, just like in the story.","concept_key":"c5-science-magnet-poles","scene_prompt":"A girl discovers that both ends of a magnet pull metal, each in its own special way."}
